i='TCS-2428';_,c=card(i);refs=c['references'];refs[0]['locator']='ITCS 2023, article 77: Equation (1), Definitions 2.1–2.3, 2.9 and 2.14–2.16, coupled-model discussion in Section 3, and full informal Conjecture 6.2 read. Its missing conclusion concerns probability laws of all multi-overlaps, beyond the proved support/overlap-gap correspondence.'
refs.append(dict(id='csp_alg2026',title='Local Algorithms and the Failure of Log-Depth Quantum Advantage on Sparse Random CSPs',authors='Antares Chen, Neng Huang, Kunal Marwaha',year=2026,url='https://doi.org/10.4230/LIPIcs.APPROX/RANDOM.2026.78',locator='RANDOM 2026, published 9 September 2026: Assumption 1 and Theorems 5–6 read. Subject to a variational-minimizer existence assumption, proves an algorithmic value guarantee for predicates without linear Fourier weight; even predicates give a match to the overlap-concentrated algorithm barrier. Does not claim convergence of full near-optimal overlap laws.'))
issue='Conjecture 6.2 is explicitly informal. It does not define the probability law for selecting near-optimal solutions, the size and scaling of the optimality window, the topology of convergence, or whether convergence is averaged over disorder or for typical disorder. These choices and the coupling/limit conventions must be fixed. Uniform sampling from a near-optimal level set and low-temperature Gibbs sampling are not silently identified.'
save(i,dict(title='Do random CSPs and their spin glasses have the same overlap laws?',formal='For a Boolean Max-CSP with random literal signs and its associated mixed Ising spin glass, do the distributions of the full overlap vectors of randomly chosen near-optimal solutions have limits as the number n of variables tends to infinity, and does the CSP limit tend to the spin-glass limit as the clause density α then tends to infinity? The source asks for this correspondence also for coupled collections of instances, but leaves the sampling and convergence conventions informal.',definitions='For a fixed arity k and predicate f:{−1,+1}ᵏ→{0,1}, generate M independent constraints on n Boolean spins: each constraint chooses k variable indices independently and uniformly from {1,…,n}, allowing repetitions, and k independent uniform signs. Its value on σ is f(ε₁σᵢ₁,…,εₖσᵢₖ). Write α=M/n for the clause density and v(σ) for the fraction of constraints satisfied. The constant mean is μ=2^{−k}Σₓf(x). For S⊆{1,…,k}, the Fourier coefficient is f̂(S)=2^{−k}Σₓ f(x)∏ⱼ∈S xⱼ. Define aₚ=Σ_{|S|=p}f̂(S)² and ξ(t)=Σ_{p=1}^k aₚtᵖ. The corresponding spin-glass objective is Hξ(σ)=Σ_{p=1}^k √aₚ n^{−(p−1)/2}Σ_{i₁,…,iₚ=1}^n J^{(p)}_{i₁,…,iₚ}σᵢ₁⋯σᵢₚ, with all J entries independent standard normal variables. The CSP improvement over its mean is on the 1/√α scale, whereas Hξ/n has constant-order optimum. For ℓ configurations σ¹,…,σℓ, their full overlap vector Q has one coordinate for every I⊆{1,…,ℓ}: Q_I=n^{−1}Σ_{j=1}^n∏_{i∈I}σⁱⱼ. The empty product is one; two-element I give ordinary pairwise overlaps, and higher-order coordinates must not be discarded. Coupled ensembles permit correlations between instance objectives, as opposed to always taking independently generated instances. The intended comparison takes the large-n limit before the large-density limit; the source does not specify all additional limiting and solution-selection choices.',question_type='yes_no',answer_criterion='A complete formulation must fix the coupled instance law, near-optimal solution distribution and accuracy window, the mode of convergence and the order of every limit. The substantive target is convergence of the full probability laws of Q, or a refutation under those same conventions. Agreement only of optimal values, attainable overlap supports or forbidden overlap regions does not establish equality of probability laws.',context_blocks=[b('Overlap records how solutions agree','Pairwise overlap is the average product of two assignments’ bits. The full vector also records joint agreement patterns among every subset of a collection of solutions.'),b('The sparse and Gaussian objectives are linked by the predicate','The associated spin-glass mixture uses the Fourier weight of each nonconstant degree of f. The random literal signs are part of the CSP model; omitting them changes the correspondence.', 'primary'),b('The source already proves weaker forms of correspondence','The paper relates optimal values and generalized overlap-gap properties at large clause density. Those statements concern where good solutions can occur, and do not determine how much probability the solution-selection law assigns to each region.', 'primary'),b('September 2026 progress concerns attainable algorithmic values','Under a stated variational-minimizer existence assumption, the later paper gives matching algorithmic values for an overlap-concentrated class in the even-predicate setting. It does not replace the missing assertion about full distributions of near-optimal solutions.', 'csp_alg2026'),b('The unresolved formulation choice',issue)],why='Would strengthen the connection between random constraint optimization and spin glasses from optimal values and solution-space supports to the actual probability law of near-optimal geometry.',references=refs,progress=[dict(date='2023',text='The source proves optimal-value and overlap-gap correspondences, then states full overlap-law convergence as an informal conjecture.',citation='primary'),dict(date='2026-09-09',text='RANDOM 2026 gives conditional algorithmic value guarantees using the CSP/spin-glass connection, without asserting the full overlap-law conjecture.',citation='csp_alg2026')],status='source_open',status_note='The full conjecture and associated model definitions were read, the January 2023 arXiv revision checked, and later-result searches include the September 2026 RANDOM paper. No precise full-law resolution was located through 12 September 2026; the original convergence statement remains explicitly informal.',model_self_contained=False,requires_context=True,statement_review=dict(reviewed_on=DATE,status='needs_specification',remaining_issue=issue,notes=['Recovered the missing distributional conclusion and exact multi-overlap vector, without inventing the probability law or convergence notion.']),importance=dict(score=c['importance']['score'],method='editorial',reason='A proposed distributional universality principle for the geometry of random optimization. Existing numerical score retained.',assessed_on=DATE),review_note='Reconstructed the missing conclusion, Boolean signed-constraint model, Fourier mixture and all subset overlaps. Kept the genuine sampling/convergence dependencies explicit and distinguished recent algorithmic progress.',working_summary=dict(sentences=['The source associates each signed Boolean Max-CSP predicate with a Gaussian spin-glass model.','It conjectures that randomly sampled near-optimal solutions have matching limiting overlap distributions.','The overlap vector includes correlations among every subset of the sampled solutions.','Known results relate optimal values and overlap gaps, which do not determine full probability distributions.','The solution-selection law, optimality window and convergence notion still require specification.'],basis='saved_sources',source_basis='source-text',written_on=DATE,source_refs=['primary','csp_alg2026'])),notes=['Recovered the full informal Conjecture 6.2 instead of treating its introductory sentence as a problem.','Defined random literal signs, Fourier weights, Gaussian normalization and all subset overlaps.','Separated full probability-law convergence from support and value theorems.','Checked September 2026 algorithmic progress and its additional assumption.'],checked_sources=[r['url'] for r in refs]+['https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.03006'],state='improved_unfinished')
